WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.040
My name's Anne-Marie Imafidon,

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.760
and I wear
a number of different hats.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.320
I'm the author of She's In CTRL.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.520
Now, Dr Anne-Marie Imafidon, who's
CEO and co-founder of the Stemettes,

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:26.920
which aims to inspire and promote
the next generation of young women

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.920
in science, technology,
engineering and maths.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:33.320
And I do lots of other
different bits and pieces.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.080
The things that land in my inbox,
people are like,

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.160
"Hey, there's this show called
Countdown. Have you heard of it?

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:40.800
"Do you want to do the numbers
for a bit?"

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.480
It's Dr Anne-Marie Imafidon!

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.320
Very nice to have you here.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:47.720
Er, sure!

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.400
Dr Anne-Marie is a computing
and mathematics genius

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:52.080
who speaks five languages.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:54.640
She got two GCSEs
while still at primary school

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.640
and is the president of
the British Science Association.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.760
If it looks like there's a plan,

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.760
then that's very interesting
for me to learn.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:09.440
Are you special?

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:10.560
I don't think so.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:16.640
I think I... Or I'll put it this
way, I think everyone's special.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:19.280
I just don't think
most folks have figured out

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:20.640
what that special is.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.560
There's an element of the way
I've been able to take opportunities

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.280
and not be afraid to try things
and fail and succeed

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.320
that maybe is special. There are
a lot of things I can't do,

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.800
a lot of things I don't remember!

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.760
Yeah, we've always only been east,
never lived anywhere else.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.360
Like, our whole lives
are centred around here.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.440
I just love the difference.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:50.160
Like, there's just...
Everybody is here and coexisting

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:53.680
and enjoying life together.
Best place on Earth!

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.800
East London. Yay!

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.000
I am the eldest of five.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:04.200
Sadly.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.800
You don't choose it, do you?

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.040
No, because if you're first,
you're the guinea pig.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.440
- I have many regrets for that.
- No, you don't!

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.080
About 62 different regrets.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:23.280
So, school was frustrating,
in part because of the repetition.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:25.880
Education is repetitive,
because that's how most folks learn.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.760
And so it was frustrating -
"We did this last year.

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:29.320
"And I remember
I was doing it last year

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.160
"and it really made sense last year,
so we can do something else."

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.560
Then it just turned into, "I'm just
going to sit and talk to Tola,

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:35.880
I'm just going to sit
and talk to Michael,

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:37.000
I'm going to sit
and talk to Rebecca."

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:41.880
I realised,
"Oh, she finishes the work early

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.600
and then she gets into mischief.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.600
"So we have to make sure
that we see how we can get rid

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:48.800
of that mischievous behaviour."

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:52.080
Every child is gifted and talented

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:56.920
in ways and manners that I may not
know, you may not know.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:00.640
So let's find out
the talent of each child.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:04.800
Outside, it was exploration.
It was like, "Let's try this.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:06.760
"There's a book that we've found
that's done this,"

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:09.560
or, "Your cousin's doing such
and such a subject. Let's try that."

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.040
At one point, I did Greek lessons,

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:13.040
did Chinese lessons,
learnt Mandarin.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.280
There were just a lot of
other things that were free

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:19.920
and available outside.
And then also I like devices.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:25.000
We had a VCR player at home. We had
lots of different things at home.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:27.520
And I was incredibly curious.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:31.880
That curiosity and wanting
to understand how that works -

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:34.560
"OK, if that works that way,
what do we do with that? OK..."

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:38.680
So, I was about four
when I took apart the VCR player.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:44.600
The thing was completely...
No matter what button I pressed,

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.520
it gave me something
totally different.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:50.320
"How does it work? Why does it
always work? How did it know?"

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:51.720
And looking inside
the cassette tape,

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:53.200
you don't find
what you're looking for.

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.480
So, yeah, I'm going to go and
have a look inside the VCR player

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.040
and maybe try and lift it up

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:00.880
and use other implements
like toy soldiers to...

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:03.480
Completely ruined my VHS.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:09.440
Very expensive. About two months'
wages. Have you paid me back?

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:10.600
Sorry, Dad.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:12.960
I have. Yeah, definitely.
I've definitely paid you back.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.960
So, we used to go
to the computer market,

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:19.680
- so it was like second-hand stuff.
- Cheaper.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.800
Yeah, so a lot cheaper, like buying
floppy disks or buying motherboards.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:24.360
See, then you get
those bits and pieces

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.480
and then try and tinker and put them
together and build a whole computer.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:28.920
Yeah, she would always be tinkering,
till she was nine or 10.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.200
I gave it a go.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:43.960
When I graduated from Oxford,

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:46.880
the plan was to go
to the City and work.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.280
It ended up being kind of
working on the tech

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.320
for the people that were in tech.
I mean, I can say it now, but, yeah,

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:55.360
I'd sit on the computer all day
for free!

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:57.520
Didn't really need to pay me
to do that.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:01.720
But I enjoyed it, because
I've always been very curious

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.160
and always been like, "We can
use technology to solve problems."

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.760
And this was like a ready supply
of problems that needed

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:08.680
to be solved with technology.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:12.600
It's because gender-diverse teams
make better decisions.

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:15.800
I ended up being invited
to speak at a conference

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.040
for 3,500 technical women
in one space.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:21.560
And as someone that's always been
in technical spaces

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.040
but never been in
female-majority technical spaces,

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:26.480
it was quite alarming for me,

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.680
quite life-affirming, as well,

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:33.280
that, "Wow! I didn't even know
I was in this minority."

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:37.040
It was almost like X-Men.
You're like, "You have powers, too?"

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:39.160
And so I ended up there

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.960
and had a bit of
a road to Damascus moment of...

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.240
No, I mean, you really are the only
girl in a lot of these spaces,

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.040
and that's kind of not OK.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:49.680
And so I ended up starting Stemettes

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:52.040
as a result of being
at that conference.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:02.400
The dream is to work
in the space sector.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.960
I am a first-year
aerospace undergraduate.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.800
Biochemistry and, like,
science to technology.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:09.640
Technology and financial markets.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.160
It can be so isolating.
You can feel so alone

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:16.640
that, actually,
just a little bit of effort

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:20.600
to get folks together pays so many
dividends for what they do next,

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:22.840
for, you know, how they see
themselves, the fact that

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:25.640
they're not the only girl in the
entire world that's ever done this

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:28.000
and will ever do it.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:30.920
It's a frustration
I do end up having with events

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:33.240
that I'm invited to these days
that are kind of 'manels', right,

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:35.760
where they're not being imaginative
or thought at all about

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:37.960
who they have
contributing to the conversation.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:39.840
And there was an email I got
literally last week

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:41.640
that had the same thing.
I had to write back and be like,

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:45.360
"Is it me, or do you not have
a single woman here on this email?

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:47.800
"And we're talking about
society in the future?

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:51.520
"And I would have thought that maybe
we might have a couple of women

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:53.240
in society in the future.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.280
"So we might want a little bit
of their experiences now

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:58.400
to be reflected
in what we're discussing.

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:00.400
"Maybe just me, I don't know!"

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:03.760
In the STEM curriculum
at GCSE and at A-level,

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:07.200
there is a lot of male names
codified in.

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:10.120
So if we say, like, I don't know,
Newton's Laws of Motion,

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:12.200
that means Isaac Newton is
inside the curriculum, is something

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:15.360
that everyone has to learn about.
And so we're wanting to just counter

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:17.080
the balance, so that
for every time there's a male name

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:18.840
you have a female
or otherwise name.

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:22.000
So whether it's GPS
and Dr Gladys West,

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:24.960
who did the maths
on the geostatic satellites,

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:28.360
whether it's Hedy Lamarr,
who was a Hollywood actress

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:30.480
and was an incredibly keen physicist

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:33.280
and ended up co-inventing
Wi-Fi or Bluetooth,

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:37.800
Katherine Johnson, who was a
mathematician in the 1960s at NASA,

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:42.520
did the maths by hand on
early space travel or space flight,

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:45.000
like Rosalind Franklin,
the women of Bletchley Park.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:48.800
There's so many! For every name that
we have already in the curriculum,

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.640
let's just put a little bit
of that 'herstory' in alongside it.

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:54.320
And it frustrates me that
we still have a national curriculum,

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:55.880
at least in this country,

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:59.080
that doesn't mention the names
of any of these women.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:03.120
And we have the audacity to think
that these dead white dudes

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:06.680
with beards are the only people
that have done anything of note.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:10.040
The audacity.

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:13.320
How dare we!

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:25.240
At the moment, with technology,
we're the entry point

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:28.800
to what folks are calling
the 4th industrial revolution.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:30.760
So if you line up eight people,

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:33.720
five of them are online
or have a mobile device,

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:36.360
which speaks a lot to
the size and the scale

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:37.920
of what we're talking about

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:40.520
but the fact that
it's not all-inclusive.

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:45.080
There are many reasons
why we're not all fluent in tech.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:47.240
There's something of
that definition of fluency.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:49.760
Being able to understand
is different to being able to speak.

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:52.680
It's different to
being able to write.

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:55.960
And it used to be reading,
writing and 'rithmetic,

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:00.200
and now it's reading, writing,
'rithmetic and r'digital.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:03.880
In access, there's access
to opportunities, but there's also

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:07.520
kind of norms, stereotypes.

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:09.840
The one that's the most frustrating

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:13.080
is that barrier of
who is able and who is capable.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:15.480
It's built on a lot of
very intentional

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:19.560
kind of social engineering
centuries - if not decades - back,

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:21.600
where if you're a young woman,

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:25.280
for example, in a classroom, if
it's technical, if it's physics,

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:28.520
if it's mathematical
or if it's engineering-related,

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:30.960
the idea that that's something
you might not get just by virtue

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:33.200
of who you are and the fact
that you... It's really hard

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:35.320
for you to actually see
that any other woman has ever done

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:38.520
maths or physics, engineering
or technology before you.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:40.840
All of that then gets in the way
of building fluency.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:44.000
But actually, if we solved that,
we would allow so many more people

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:46.680
to build that fluency
and allow so many more people

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:50.320
to then have agency
and also solve so many problems.

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:56.120
I am the new chair
of UD Music Foundation.

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:04.240
UD is here in east London
and is all about opening up

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:08.200
opportunities in a way that folks,
especially from this area,

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:10.520
might not have
seen or observed or be aware

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:13.400
that that is value
that they can bring to this space.

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:15.800
Anne-Marie first came to us,

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:19.560
and she was part of a scheme
called Young Board Members.

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:20.760
Roll it round, roll it round.

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:26.720
Wow! Energy, intelligence, insight,
drive, passion, leadership.

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:28.640
That's how I'd describe her.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:36.160
The focus on music technology tends
to be embedded within the kind of

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:39.920
songwriting and production process.
We need to build the skill set

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:43.080
that is going to make
people's careers fit for purpose.

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:46.080
She's a detail person
so gets under the bonnet.

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:51.520
That detailed knowledge and insight
on how technology

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:54.560
is changing people's lives -
visionary.

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:06.200
A-Lab or AID Lab is the Artificial
Intelligence Design Laboratory,

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:09.040
and as far as we know,
it's the world's first dedicated

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:13.160
laboratory to bringing together
design with artificial intelligence.

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:16.480
The folks at Royal College of Art,
like, that, that is really,

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:19.440
really cutting-edge. A lot of folks
don't get to see that,

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:21.520
don't even imagine that a lot
of those things are possible

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:23.880
or that that would be
a problem you want to solve.

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:28.600
We look at transforming bio-waste
into new materials and textiles.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:32.440
I still love the technology. I love
the problems that can be solved.

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:34.960
And I'm also incredibly curious
about things.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:37.200
Anne-Marie's a member of our council
at the Royal College of Art.

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:40.000
She's a great champion
and supporter of what we do.

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:42.640
Whenever I talk with Anne-Marie,
there's amazing curiosity.

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:46.320
Whereas being able to
have it then choose is brilliant,

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:48.640
and why not be inspired by nature?

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:51.160
There's no holds barred
about asking the questions.

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:55.240
They're very considered questions,
very thoughtful interventions

00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:57.800
about what is the direction
of research policy,

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:01.160
why are we doing what we do,
also about how do we make sure

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:04.560
that technology and STEM
is available for everyone,

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:08.520
it represents everyone and brings
everyone into that conversation.

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:12.520
She's really someone who's writing
the future and enabling people

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:15.400
to be part of that future
and write it, as well.

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:20.280
I think I'm very conscious
that I am a young...

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:24.240
..black woman from east London who's
had a very different upbringing

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:26.560
from a lot of people
at these board tables,

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:29.200
who spends a lot of time
with young people.

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:34.000
And so there's a lot of
insights, experiences, stories

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:37.720
that I get to hear that then can be
fed back at those top levels.

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:42.120
And so being able to bring that
to such a wide range of institutions

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:47.840
excites me, because that's kind of
my way of doing the systemic change,

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:50.040
attack from the bottom
and attack from the top.

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:51.560
And that's kind of what I'm doing.

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:54.560
And you've got to learn the system
to be able to change it.

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:56.760
- Hello!
- What should we do?

00:12:56.800 --> 00:13:00.320
Hey, let's do that.
In your brand-new home town.

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:02.840
I know! I've a new job
and everything!

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:04.360
Yeah. It's number 17.

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:06.760
I just want you to get the tattoo
or the T-shirt or something.

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:08.120
Or "Number one, first woman."

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:10.360
I think we'll get you
a number one T-shirt.

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:12.200
We'll make you a number one T-shirt.

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:14.360
Am I dancing inside
that there's a woman at the table?

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:17.160
Yes, I'm dancing inside
because, for a lot of folks,

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:19.520
if you can't see it,
you can't be it.

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:23.520
I'm dancing because if she's able
to occupy that position,

00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:26.720
who else could occupy
other positions?

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:29.720
Why have I met
so many directors at GCHQ?

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:31.320
Classified information!

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:36.040
Only joking.
You'll have to ask them.

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:38.360
I have a hunch
that in 20 years' time,

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:40.680
when we look back on Anne-Marie,

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:45.080
she will be seen as someone
who has led the charge.

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:48.120
I think she is destined
for even greater things.

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:52.800
I think her dad wants her to be
prime minister. Let's see.

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:55.480
I'd love to be remembered
as being a big part of that move

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:59.120
or that change
towards who is technical,

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:01.280
what does technical look like.

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:05.080
I have no idea what's next.
Also, I'm still curious.

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:08.720
This is basically me
opening the VCR player,

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:11.320
but just in
a slightly different way.

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:14.680
I could literally be
sat here for hours.

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:17.120
Yeah!

